aurora-speaker-upgrade on Aurora, based on Debian testing
=========================================================

speakerguardd v2 is opt in. A fresh install keeps the legacy speaker
protection on the Macs it supports: speakersafetyd (with
speakersafetyd-aurora-profiles) and, on the MacBook Neo (J700), speakerguardd
0.1. The kernel holds every other Mac's speakers at their safe level.

The legacy protection is deprecated and will be removed. Please help bring up
speakerguardd v2 on your Mac:

  sudo aurora-speaker-upgrade --check   # dry run: collect, compile, self-check
  sudo aurora-speaker-upgrade           # switch, only if the self-check passes
  sudo aurora-speaker-upgrade status
  sudo aurora-speaker-upgrade --revert  # back to the legacy protection
  aurora-speaker-upgrade guide          # when Linux cannot read the tuning

and report the result at https://github.com/aurora-silicon/speakerguardd/issues.

aurora-speaker-upgrade.service is installed disabled. Enabling it
(systemctl enable aurora-speaker-upgrade.service) makes every boot try the
switch, as alarm-aurora and fedora-aurora do; a refusal is recorded and not
retried until the kernel, speakerguardd v2 or the tool changes.

While a legacy daemon holds the speakers, a notice says so at console and SSH
logins (/etc/update-motd.d/60-aurora-speaker-protection) and once per user in
the desktop (/etc/xdg/autostart/aurora-speaker-legacy-notice.desktop). Remove
either file to silence it.
